# The shelf's made-up creatures described in the language: numbers, plurals and shapes.
lesson language-my-monster v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=7 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[describe.my-monster|cap]]"
  goal "Describe a made-up creature in [[language.name]] by its eyes, legs, horns, shape and coat."

  look {
    scene 31x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        creatures line eyes=[1, 2, 3, 2] legs=[2, 4, 2, 6] horns=[0, 2, 1, 0] coat=[0, 1, 2, 1] body=[0, 1, 2, 0] colors=["sky", "mint", "berry", "tang"] labels=["A", "B", "C", "D"] ring=-1
      }
    }
    say """
      Describe a creature by what it has: [[describe.it-has-two-eyes]] (two eyes),
      [[describe.it-has-four-legs]] (four legs), [[describe.it-has-one-horn]] (one horn). Its coat
      can have [[describe.stripes]] (stripes) or [[describe.spots]] (spots). Its body can be round:
      [[describe.it-is-round]] or long: [[describe.it-is-long]]
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.my-monster-name k=4
    }
    show language.my-monster-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.my-monster-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.my-monster-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.my-monster-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.my-monster-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.my-monster-write k=5 easy-k=3 hard-k=4
    show language.my-monster-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.my-monster-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.my-monster-write k=3
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-my-monster-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.my-monster-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[describe.it-has-two-eyes]] [[describe.it-has-four-legs]] [[describe.it-is-round]] Count, then say."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is a number with only one of a thing (three eye), and a describing
    word that does not match, which the two-star question asks about. The creatures are drawn so
    that each feature can be counted or seen, and colour never decides an answer, so the page reads
    the same in print. From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed
    without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read
    aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, count each feature aloud in the language,
    pointing. Off the page, draw a monster together and write three sentences about it. It meets
    England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "understand
    basic grammar appropriate to the language being studied, including (where relevant): feminine,
    masculine and neuter forms"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can write about
    themselves and familiar and simple matters by using simple words and phrases and basic
    expressions for which they are sufficiently familiar with the sounds while refering to example
    sentences"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic
    phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[describe.it-is-round]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[describe.it-has-two-eyes]] and [[describe.it-has-stripes]] and the words to write are [[describe.legs-of-an-animal]] and [[describe.spots]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[describe.it-has-one-horn]] and [[describe.it-is-long]] and the words to write are [[describe.horns]], [[describe.round]] and [[describe.spots]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
